From: Brian.Hawthorne@East.Sun.COM (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: RECON: Free-Market vets

 
> That is an interesting way to look at it. Has anyone seen any comparison
> between quality of care between vets and human docs? Mortality rates? 
> I have always been surprised at how cheaply vets supply medical services in
> comparison to doctors. 

I suspect that vets costs are much lower. In particular, they need almost
no liability insurance, since people rarely sue for malpractice on their
pet, and courts are unlikely to award huge pain-and-suffering awards.

Also, patient expectations are much lower. If your cat has a malignant
tumor on its foot, the vet will recommend amputation. Humans would find
this unacceptable. If a vet resets a broken hip and the dog walks with
a limp for the rest of his life, its owner will probably be satisfied.
If I break my hip and get it repaired, I want perfect functioning
afterward. 

Also, because most pets have much shorter lifespans than humans (usually
less than 25 years), some therapies are available that would often be
avoided for humans. For instance, many of the drugs used to treat disease
in pets are extremely carcinogenic. However, since the pet is only going 
to live another 10 or 20 years, it is unlikely to have time to develop
the cancer from long-term use. 

These are just a few reasons for the low cost of animal health care. I do
not doubt, though that the lack of an AMA or major government restrictions
helps substantially.

Rowan Hawthorne

