>      XXXXXXXXX            - YYYYYYYY

>   The above are some of the biggest advancements in the industrial age.
>I would fill in Computer Science/Chemistry - Nanotechnology. Klaus! might
>fill in Number Theory - Public Key Encryption. Anyone have any others
>that might fit this list?

>                Two future possibilities (far far far future)
>
>           Theory of Everything -  Cosmic/Gravitational engineering
>              Extropianism      -    Omniscience(potence)

Speaking as Tim, and not Klaus! (my nom de joke), I would suggest:

computer science  ----->   virtual realities
computer science, other areas ----> artificial intelligence (of some form)
number theory/math -----> crypto anarchy, digital privacy
systems/chaos/information theory -----> new outlooks on complex systems

I don't expect any current work in "basic" physics (Theory of Everything,
cosmic strings, superstrings, inflationary universe, etc.) to have _any_
real-world, engineering application any time in the next several decades.
The stuff is just too abstruse, too outre. 

(What I mean by this is that while QM dealt with "everyday" things, like
the behavior of electrons in solids, the spectrum of the sun and other
sources, superconductivity, etc., modern particle physics has essentially
no links to anything tangible, in laboratories. The quark theory, for
example, has not--and likely will not--result in any new devices or
gadgets.)

As my list above suggests, the part of physics dealing with complex systems
(all the trendy stuff in self-organizing behavior, fractals and chaos,
etc.) will likely combine with similar stuff out of economics (markets,
agoric systems, a la Hayek) and out of information theory (predictability,
complexity, coding) to form an interesting new technology. Arguably this is
already happening.

I expect the sequencing of DNA, for example, to be relatively
straightforward. Understanding the _meaning_ of the sequence (in the sense
of interpreting the code, the words) will be a bigger challenge.

Artificial intelligence is in a similar position. Understanding the
language of brains, the symbol systems and pathways, will be a fascinating
challenge.

In my opinion, these are the areas of excitement and synthesis.

Other views?

-Tim May

