From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: RECON: Free-Market vets

(I've lost my Extropian Secret Keyword Decoder RIng--what does "RECON" mean?)

Other and various reasons for vets costing so much less than human doctors:

* Many seriously ill animals are euthanized, which is not an option with
humans. Since much of the cost of medical care is for terminal patients (I
have heard the factoid: "70% of medical care costs go to patients in the
last 6 months of their life," but I can't quote a source for this.), this
seems to explain a big chunk of why human cost care is so expensive. 

* Pet insurance is a rarity. Thus consumers, like Ray, are likely to be
price-sensitive. In the human health care business, people routinely
"demand the best" and fight a battle with their insurance companies and
their claims investigators to have all kinds of treatments covered. (ABC's
"NIghtline" last night reported on something I'd not heard mentioned:
people go to Mexico for bogus cancer cures, plastic surgery, and the like,
and then have it billed as coverable expenses. For example, someone wanting
an elective "nose job" claims to have fallen in Puerto Vallarta and needed
emergency nose surgery...the insurance company has little recourse but to
pay,since emergency care is covered.)

* With humans, the cost of unreimbursed care (emergency rooms, trauma
centers, deadbeats, the indigent, etc.) is amortized over all paying
patients. This is not the case with pet care.

(Though many vets do volunteer work, public service spaying and neutering,
etc., and this in some way affects their income and hence their desired
fees. A minor part of doing business, I suspect, rather than a wholesale
subsidy.)

* The points made by others about different standards for health care. We
demand more for human care, and so long as "the insurance companies" are
paying, we demand the best. Minnesota has more NMR and CAT scanners than
all of Canada does (not that I'm advocating the Canadian style of health
care).

* Different training requirements. Vets are lower paid than doctors.

* Pet facilities are smaller, with less capital equipment. Much more like
the doctors offices of years ago.

A fascinating comparison.

-Tim
