Date: Fri, 26 Feb 1993 15:15-EST
From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Hal>  My problem was, I came up with a bet with a negative log return but a
Hal>  positive linear return: a 60% chance that your money would halve, and
Hal>  a 40% chance that your money would double.

On any bet with a positive linear return on any given bet, there's
always a way to get a positive log return on your bankroll.  The trick
is to make your bet size a small fraction of the bankroll.

Let's figure out what bet size to use for this game, in order to
maximize your log return.  We must solve for f, the correct fraction of
your bankroll to bet:  (bankroll is B)

     want to maximize log(B-fB/2)*0.6 + log(B+fB)*0.4

              0   =  d/df ( log(B-fB/2)*0.6 + log(B+fB)*0.4 )

                       -0.6*B       0.4*B
              0   =     -----    +  -----
                      2*(B-fB/2)    B+fB

	0.6(B+fB) = 0.4(2B-fB)
        0.6(1+f)  = 0.4(2-f)
             f    =  0.2

So unless I've blown the math, you should bet 0.2 times your bankroll
on each bet in order to maximize log return.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

