Date: Sat, 27 Feb 93 09:52:13 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: IDEN: Ants and the Brain Boost

Hal writes:
> Imagine somehow engineering an ant to become a human: perhaps
> transplanting its miniscule bit of neural tissue into the middle of
> a human brain, then teaching that human just as you would a baby until
> it grows into a productive member of society.  Is it still the same
> identity as the ant?
> I would say not.

Put like that most people would say: the ant is dead.  But I think this
is a skewed example.  I don't remember being a baby.  Perhaps I was an
ant, but I don't know it!  Suppose, instead, this transformation process
preserved memory (as surely future self transformation will, except by
volition).  The ant takes the Brain Boost and becomes sapient.  When
questioned the super-ant can recall its past.  Is this the same ant?
I would say so.

Unless there is some fundamental why brain-boosting erases pre-boost
memories then (by my personal criteria) identity is preserved.  By
analogy with neural nets we can guess memories would be lost in a fixed-
sized net as old synaptic weights are overwritten.  ie new memories
degrade old memories.  But in an expanding neural net (new neurons) it
would be possible to fix some of the weights (ie retain ancient
memories) and not impact on the performance of the whole net to learn
new tricks.

So I reach diametrically the opposite conclusion to Hal:

> But growth and death are two sides of the same coin.  By committing
> to growth we agree to the death of the person we are today, in the 
> hopes that we will be a better person tomorrow.

Growth and death are alternatives.  Only by perpetual growth can we
preserve our past memories, identities etc and still remain dynamic. (Is
than an oxymoron?).  Otherwise, like Sherlock Holmes, the attics of our
mind becomes cluttered with the cobwebs of our past.

> Hal Finney
> 

