Date:     Sat, 27 Feb 93 11:30:00 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Duelling Vets

Alright, this post won't actually be about duelling vets, but I couldn't
resist a more amusing title.  And, to deter complaints, I have no idea
what prefix this should be filed under, so don't complain, suggest!

I'm always highly amused when a brief comment of mine sparks a nice long
thread.  I liked it when my comment about aircars making freeways largely
obsolete did it, and I like it that my comment about dueling has done it.
Thank you all!

There seem to be a couple of confusions about dueling, though.  One is the:
take it back or I'll beat you up! objection.  The one challenged has no
obligation to either retract the claim or blithely accept being beat up
if she's unwilling (or able) to fight.  Suppose I call you a flying yogi;
you tell me to take it back, or you'll beat me up.  If I don't want to
fight you or take it back, I can just decline the challenge.  If you
proceed to beat me up, I can have you prosecuted for an intentional tort.

Another one is that duelling favors those who are skilled in martial arts.
What makes this more complicated than it first appears is that the one
challenged has the choice of weapons, or more.  Lord Byron wasn't a very
good swordsman due to his club foot, so he made himself a good shot to
compensate.  If he was challenged to a duel, you bet he would choose pistols
over swords if he wanted to have his best chance at winning.  This is an
example of how different people are skilled at different martial arts.  The
challenger always runs the risk of having to fight in their opponent's
specialty while not being very good at it.

Byron also challenged others to swimming contests, to prove athletic ability
in spite of his foot.

As for the vets, the story of the low cost of vet care is even more interesting
in light of the fact that vets have to go to school longer than M.D.s - they
have to learn much more anatomy!  It's harder to become a vet, which means
that they oughta be able to charge more for their services.

However, vets don't seem to have been the "beneficiaries" of the cost-plus
insurance system that dominated the market for human medical care from WWII
until about 1983 (due to statist interventions, mostly tax exemptions).  That's
what has really driven up most human medical care in price - this is the true
culprit for the high price of pharmeceuticals, even, according to a recent
report by the US Federal Office of Technology Assessment.  So much for the
truth to Billary's scapegoating of that industry for why we spend too much
on medical care!

An interesting exception to cost-plus insurance coverage in human medical
care suggested in "Patient Power" (order your copy from Freedom's Forum
today and get your ISIL member's discount!) is the cosmetic surgery market.
According to the authors, there have been no studies of this market - what
an opportunity for research!  About all they say about it is that there are
apparently more dangerous procedures than breast-implants that the FDA doesn't
pay much attention to at all.  Unfortunately, they don't specify what these
might be.

So, it seems that the freest market there is in human medical care is the
cosmetic surgery market, and there hasn't been enough concern about it for
there to have been any study of it at all.  Veddy Eenterestink!

